aracteristic, nongenerator, Frattini and correspondence statements. Quotient generation gives <S,Phi(G)>=G; deleting the finite list of nongenerators individually preserves generation even if some already belong to S. Both directions hold, with G=1 and S empty; no infinite choice. No routed finding.","prior_review":{"status":"complete","reviewer":"Alpha group c (Claude Opus 5)","refuter":"alpha-c-ref-frattini (read-only proof refuter, Claude Opus 5)","notes":"REF confirmed the Statement says 'a subset S of a FINITE group G', which is the hypothesis the nongenerator argument needs, and that no proof on this page runs a 'take a maximal subgroup containing' argument outside a finite group. Alpha checked both directions and the trivial-group case at step 2.1."}},"routing":["Step-3a Alpha reads every step and cited source and records a risk_review disposition"]},{"id":"lem-elementary-abelian-p-groups-are-canonical-fp-vector-spaces","score":8,"tier":"critical","signals":[{"reason":"5 declared dependencies","points":2},{"reason":"5 cited facts","points":2},{"reason":"boundary-sensitive language","points":2},{"reason":"quotient or equivalence-class construction","points":2}],"required":true,"risk_review":{"status":"complete","reviewer":"Alpha batch-1 Step 3a","notes":"Reviewed Proof 1.1–3.1 and exact elementary-abelian, prime-field, residue-ring and integer-power laws. Since x^p=e, representative differences kp vanish for all integer k. Commutativity supplies the scalar distributive law; the listed identities are all vector axioms over the prime field. E=1 satisfies them with zero vector. No routed finding.","prior_review":{"status":"complete","reviewer":"Alpha group c (Claude Opus 5)","refuter":"alpha-c-ref-frattini (read-only proof refuter, Claude Opus 5)","notes":"Well-definedness of the scalar action is the risk. REF verified that exponent p is used at step 1.1, where x^a = x^b (x^p)^k, and that abelianness is used at step 2.1 through the commuting clause of lem-group-power-laws; both conditions come from the definition and neither is assumed silently. Alpha confirmed the trivial group is permitted by def-elementary-abelian-p-group and survives."}},"routing":["Step-3a Alpha reads every step and cited source and records a risk_review disposition"]},{"id":"lem-finite-elementary-abelian-p-groups-have-bases-and-basis-extension","score":10,"tier":"critical","signals":[{"reason":"6 declared dependencies","points":2},{"reason":"6 cited facts","points":2},{"reason":"boundary-sensitive language","points":2},{"reason":"induction, recursion, or minimality","points":2},{"reason":"quotient or equivalence-class construction","points":2}],"required":true,"risk_review":{"status":"complete","reviewer":"Alpha batch-1 Step 3a","notes":"Reviewed Proof 1.1–3.1 and exact basis, product-count, canonical prime-factorisation, well-ordering, residue-cardinality and natural-power statements. Minimal spanning subsets are independent by solving one nonzero coefficient. Finite extension terminates and deletion inside S preserves spanning. Coordinate bijection gives |E|=p^|B|; prime-exponent uniqueness yields equal sizes including empty bases and E=1. Only finitely many choices; no AC.","prior_review":{"status":"complete","reviewer":"Alpha group c (Claude Opus 5)","refuter":"alpha-c-ref-frattini (read-only proof refuter, Claude Opus 5)","notes":"Alpha checked all four clauses are proved: existence of a basis, extension of an independent subset, containment of a basis in a spanning subset, and equal finite size. REF confirmed the extension clause is applied elsewhere on the page only in the direction it is stated, three times, including to a basis of a subspace inside the ambient space."}},"routing":["Step-3a Alpha reads every step and cited source and records a risk_review disposition"]},{"id":"thm-frattini-quotient-is-the-largest-elementary-abelian-quotient","score":10,"tier":"critical","signals":[{"reason":"12 declared dependencies","points":3},{"reason":"10 cited facts","points":2},{"reason":"biconditional / both-direction claim","points":3},{"reason":"quotient or equivalence-class construction","points":2}],"required":true,"risk_review":{"status":"complete","reviewer":"Alpha batch-1 Step 3a","notes":"Read all three steps and exact 12 dependency statements. Maximal quotients are C_p; intersecting their kernels kills commutators and pth powers. P/N and P/Phi(P) are finite p-groups since |P|=[P:N]|N| and each positive factor of p^n is a p-power. A nonzero coset extends to a basis, whose complementary span lifts to a maximal subgroup excluding x; this proves Phi(P)<=N. Reverse follows by killing powers and commutators; P=1 and N=P work. Reader/refuter 3 identify an implicit immediate order argument, confirmed nonfatal polish, not a false claim or unmet prerequisite; published text left unchanged.","prior_review":{"status":"complete","reviewer":"Alpha group c (Claude Opus 5)","refuter":"alpha-c-ref-frattini (read-only proof refuter, Claude Opus 5)","notes":"REF finding C-FR-3, adjudicated confirmed_nonfatal by Alpha and repaired here rather than deferred. Step 2.1 credited thm-correspondence-theorem-groups with the isomorphism (P/Phi(P))/(N/Phi(P)) = P/N, which is the third isomorphism theorem and is neither in deps nor cited. Alpha rewrote step 2.1 to reach the same conclusion directly: step 1.1 already places every commutator and every p-th power inside Phi(P), hence inside N, so P/N is abelian of exponent dividing p. The stale L3 use at 2.1 was removed from the contract. The forward direction at 1.2 was re-derived independently, including the one-dimensional case where the omitted-basis span is the zero subspace."}},"routing":["Step-3a Alpha reads every step and cited source and records a risk_review disposition"]},{"id":"thm-frattini-subgroup-formula-for-a-finite-p-group","score":8,"tier":"critical","signals":[{"reason":"5 declared dependencies","points":2},{"reason":"4 cited facts","points":2},{"reason":"boundary-sensitive language","points":2},{"reason":"quotient or equivalence-class construction","points":2}],"required":true,"risk_review":{"status":"complete","reviewer":"Alpha batch-1 Step 3a","notes":"Read Proof 1.1–3.1 and power-subgroup, largest-quotient, commutator-normality and normal-product interfaces. Automorphisms permute powers bijectively, giving characteristic P^p; normal factors give normal K. Elementary quotient gives K<=Phi(P). Conversely P/K is a finite p-group by quotient cardinality dividing p^n, abelian and killed by p, so the kernel criterion gives Phi(P)<=K. Includes P=1. Reader/refuter 4 concern the implicit quotient-order observation; confirmed nonfatal polish. Checked Craven Prop 2.24–2.25; source has explanatory slips (powers described as elements of order p and reversed final inclusion); current local definition and inclusion are correct.","prior_review":{"status":"complete","reviewer":"Alpha group c (Claude Opus 5)","refuter":"alpha-c-ref-frattini (read-only proof refuter, Claude Opus 5)","notes":"REF finding C-FR-4, adjudicated confirmed_nonfatal by Alpha and repaired. Step 1.1 credited lem-commutator-subgroup-is-normal with the normality of P^p as well as of P', and credited lem-product-with-normal-subgroup with normality of the product when that lemma gives only that the product is a subgroup. Alpha wrote both inferences out: P^p is normal because it is characteristic and conjugation is an automorphism, and the product of two normal subgroups is normal by a one-line conjugation computation. Both inclusions of Phi(P) = P'P^p were verified independently."}},"routing":["Step-3a Alpha reads every step and cited source and records a risk_review disposition"]},{"id":"prop-frattini-subgroup-monotonicity-for-subgroups-of-finite-p-groups","score":5,"tier":"high","signals":[{"reason":"2 cited facts","points":1},{"reason":"boundary-sensitive language","points":2},{"reason":"analytic limiting/completeness language","points":2}],"required":true,"risk_review":{"status":"complete","reviewer":"Alpha batch-1 Step 3a","notes":"Reviewed both steps against formula and homomorphism/derived-series supplier. The inclusion homomorphism gives Hprime<=Pprime; generators h^p are P-powers, so H^p<=P^p. Products preserve inclusions and formula applies to both given finite p-groups. H=1, H=P, P=1 work; no unjustified general functoriality.","prior_review":{"status":"complete","reviewer":"Alpha group c (Claude Opus 5)","refuter":"alpha-c-ref-frattini (read-only proof refuter, Claude Opus 5)","notes":"Alpha checked the direction of the inclusion against the standard statement and confirmed the hypothesis is a subgroup of a finite p-group, not an arbitrary subgroup of an arbitrary group. REF confirmed no maximal-subgroup existence argument is run outside a finite group."}},"routing":["Step-3a Alpha reads every step and cited source and records a risk_review disposition"]},{"id":"prop-frattini-subgroup-of-a-quotient-finite-p-group","score":8,"tier":"critical","signals":[{"reason":"4 declared dependencies","points":2},{"reason":"4 cited facts","points":2},{"reason":"quotient or equivalence-class construction","points":2},{"reason":"analytic limiting/completeness language","points":2}],"required":true,"risk_review":{"status":"complete","reviewer":"Alpha batch-1 Step 3a","notes":"Reviewed both steps and four exact cited supplier statements. Surjectivity gives images of derived subgroup and p-power generators exactly, not merely inclusions. |P/N|=[P:N] is a positive factor of p^n by Lagrange, so formula applies; image preimage is Phi(P)N and correspondence identifies quotient. N=1,N=P hold. Refuter 13 identifies the implicit immediate order check: confirmed nonfatal polish, recorded without a source edit.","prior_review":{"status":"complete","reviewer":"Alpha group c (Claude Opus 5)","refuter":"alpha-c-ref-frattini (read-only proof refuter, Claude Opus 5)","notes":"Alpha traced the argument through the Frattini formula rather than through a correspondence of maximal subgroups, and confirmed the quotient is by a normal subgroup as required. REF checked the trivial-quotient case."}},"routing":["Step-3a Alpha reads every step and cited source and records a risk_review disposition"]},{"id":"prop-frattini-subgroup-of-a-direct-product-of-finite-p-groups","score":5,"tier":"high","signals":[{"reason":"8 declared dependencies","points":3},{"reason":"8 cited facts","points":2}],"required":true,"risk_review":{"status":"complete","reviewer":"Alpha batch-1 Step 3a","notes":"Reviewed all steps and eight exact supplier statements. Natural product count yields p^(m+n), including trivial factors. Componentwise commutators and powers give forward inclusions; reverse inclusions follow by embedding generators as (g,1) and (1,h), then multiplying coordinate factors. Normal-product formula gives the claimed equality. No choice or unsupported product-order premise.","prior_review":null},"routing":["Step-3a Alpha reads every step and cited source and records a risk_review disposition"]},{"id":"cor-frattini-subgroup-of-a-finite-two-group-is-the-square-subgroup","score":8,"tier":"critical","signals":[{"reason":"3 cited facts","points":1},{"reason":"biconditional / both-direction claim","points":3},{"reason":"boundary-sensitive language","points":2},{"reason":"quotient or equivalence-class construction","points":2}],"required":true,"risk_review":{"status":"complete","reviewer":"Alpha batch-1 Step 3a","notes":"Reviewed both proof steps and exact formula, power-subgroup and quotient-abelian criterion interfaces. P^2 is normal by the formula supplier. In its quotient every inverse equals the element, so (xy)^-1=yx=xy; then Pprime<=P^2 and formula yields equality. Trivial P also works. No flagged defect.","prior_review":{"status":"complete","reviewer":"Alpha group c (Claude Opus 5)","refuter":"alpha-c-ref-frattini (read-only proof refuter, Claude Opus 5)","notes":"Alpha checked that P' is absorbed into P^2 for a 2-group, which is what collapses the formula, and REF confirmed the P=1 case is named explicitly at step 2.1."}},"routing":["Step-3a Alpha reads every step and cited source and records a risk_review disposition"]},{"id":"cor-frattini-free-finite-p-groups-are-elementary-abelian","score":6,"tier":"high","signals":[{"reason":"2 cited facts","points":1},{"reason":"biconditional / both-direction claim","points":3},{"reason":"quotient or equivalence-class construction","points":2}],"required":true,"risk_review":{"status":"complete","reviewer":"Alpha batch-1 Step 3a","notes":"Read both steps; largest-elementary-quotient criterion with N=1 gives both directions. Phi(P)=1 identifies the quotient with P; elementary P implies Phi(P)<=1. Trivial P works and no further assumptions or choice are used.","prior_review":{"status":"complete","reviewer":"Alpha group c (Claude Opus 5)","refuter":"alpha-c-ref-frattini (read-only proof refuter, Claude Opus 5)","notes":"Both directions checked. REF confirmed the corollary survives P=1 in both directions because def-elementary-abelian-p-group permits the trivial group; Alpha confirmed the forward direction uses the largest-elementary-abelian-quotient theorem at N=1 rather than a separate argument."}},"routing":["Step-3a Alpha reads every step and cited source and records a risk_review disposition"]},{"id":"thm-burnside-basis-theorem","score":13,"tier":"critical","signals":[{"reason":"6 declared dependencies","points":2},{"reason":"5 cited facts","points":2},{"reason":"biconditional / both-direction claim","points":3},{"reason":"boundary-sensitive language","points":2},{"reason":"induction, recursion, or minimality","points":2},{"reason":"quotient or equivalence-class construction","points":2}],"required":true,"risk_review":{"status":"complete","reviewer":"Alpha batch-1 Step 3a","notes":"Read all steps and exact generation, minimal-set, rank, basis/basis-extension and elementary-quotient statements. Forward minimality excludes zero images, duplicate cosets and proper spanning subimages; basis extraction then gives a basis. Reverse injectivity ensures deleting a member deletes a basis vector, and any proper subset is contained in such a deletion. Restricted bijection/indexed formulation preserves multiplicity. P=1 has only the empty minimal set and empty basis. No AC; finite lifts suffice.","prior_review":{"status":"complete","reviewer":"Alp
